{ PTR_BITS } :: import "target.nest"

// The integer families, and the pointer-sized types defined over them.
//
// An integer is a member of one of two families (§3.1): `int.<N>` for the signed
// widths and `uint.<N>` for the unsigned ones, with `N` the width in bits. `i32`
// is `int.<32>` and `u8` is `uint.<8>` — the same type, written with the width as
// spelling instead of as an argument — so a method written here for every `N` is
// a method on every integer type at once.
//
// That is the whole reason the families exist. `u4096` is a legal type, so there
// is no finite list of widths to write `wrapping_add` out for; without a generic
// self type there is no way to give it to every integer at all. The array impl in
// `slice.nest` is the same shape with one parameter: `impl <T, const N: usize>
// [N]T` is to `[3]i32` what `impl <const N: u16> int.<N>` is to `i32`.
//
// The width parameter is a `u16` because §3.1 caps a width at 65535 — a width is
// a compile-time number and never anything else, so the smallest type that holds
// every legal one is the honest declaration.
//
// The two families are written out separately rather than as one family with a
// signedness argument. Signedness is not something anything is ever generic over
// — no operation here would read it — so making it an argument would buy an
// inference variable no program could solve, and would let a signed type and an
// unsigned one unify through it. Two impls is the honest shape; each one is a
// handful of lines.
//
// The coherence rule is why these can only live in `core`: an inherent impl may
// only be written in the package that defines the type, and the primitive types
// belong to `core` (§4.9).
//
// `Self` inside each impl is the family member being implemented — `int.<N>` for
// that same `N` — which is what lets every signature below say "same family, same
// width, no widening" without stating a bound. A `wrapping_add` that took a
// different width, or returned one, could not be written. That is also why these
// need no entry in the compiler's `Special` table: unlike `make` and `len`, whose
// rules no signature can state, everything true of these *is* in the signature.
//
// Each method is the intrinsic itself, not a wrapper around a free one. An
// `#intrinsic` declaration is an ordinary bodyless function and an impl member
// may be exactly that, so there is nothing for a wrapper to add.

// The pointer-sized integers (§3.1). They are **defined here**, not built into
// the compiler: `usize` is a `distinct` type over the unsigned integer as wide
// as a pointer, and `PTR_BITS` is what the build says that width is.
//
// `distinct` is doing the real work (§2.4). `usize` and `u64` have the same
// representation on a 64-bit machine and are still different types, so
// `impl usize` and `impl u64` cannot collide and a count of bytes cannot be
// handed to something expecting a number of them. Being a `distinct` is also
// what keeps it out of implicit widening: a `u32` reaching a `usize` is written
// down, which it has to be, because whether it fits is the target's business.
//
// The compiler finds them by tag, never by name, so a `core` that spells them
// differently still works.
@public usize :: #lang("usize") distinct uint.<PTR_BITS>
@public isize :: #lang("isize") distinct int.<PTR_BITS>

impl <const N: u16> int.<N> {
  // Wrapping arithmetic (§6.6): the result is reduced modulo 2^N, which is what
  // the machine instruction does. This is the *stated* overflow behaviour, as
  // against the `+` operator, whose overflow is a separate question the language
  // answers elsewhere — the point of the name is that a reader sees which was
  // meant at the call site.
  @public wrapping_add :: #intrinsic("wrapping_add") func (self: Self, rhs: Self) -> Self
  @public wrapping_sub :: #intrinsic("wrapping_sub") func (self: Self, rhs: Self) -> Self
  // Multiplication is here for the same reason the other two are, and it earns
  // its place on its own: a hash function is a multiply that is *meant* to
  // overflow, and under `-C overflow=trap` there is otherwise no way to write
  // one (`std/collections`).
  @public wrapping_mul :: #intrinsic("wrapping_mul") func (self: Self, rhs: Self) -> Self
}

impl <const N: u16> uint.<N> {
  @public wrapping_add :: #intrinsic("wrapping_add") func (self: Self, rhs: Self) -> Self
  @public wrapping_sub :: #intrinsic("wrapping_sub") func (self: Self, rhs: Self) -> Self
  @public wrapping_mul :: #intrinsic("wrapping_mul") func (self: Self, rhs: Self) -> Self
}
